// A Read is permitted if there was formerly a Login. Unlike case 1117, this
// policy DOES write the pinned field — but with a wildcard,
// `callerPrincipal: _`, an explicit attempt to accept a Login by ANY
// principal (a bypass of the schema's principal pin).
//
// Because the pin is injected unconditionally, the predicate ends up
// constraining `callerPrincipal` twice: the author's wildcard (matches
// anything) AND the pin's `principal` (the current request's
// principal). The wildcard adds nothing; the pin still forces the past Login
// to share the Read's principal. A hand-written field can only NARROW a pin,
// never relax it — so this bypass attempt has no effect.
//
// Positive/negative control across two traces (identical to 1117's, proving
// the wildcard changed nothing):
//   trace_1: the prior Login is by the SAME principal as the Read → permitted.
//   trace_2: IDENTICAL except the prior Login is by a DIFFERENT principal →
//            the injected pin forces a mismatch → NOT permitted.
// If the wildcard had bypassed the pin, trace_2 would wrongly permit.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user, callerPrincipal: _ }
};
